<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">José María Castro Madriz</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1818">1818</a>–<a href="/years/1892">1892</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">José María Castro Madriz (1818–1892) was a Costa Rican lawyer and statesman who as president proclaimed the Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/costa-rica">Costa Rica</a> on 31 August 1848, and is honoured as its founder. A liberal, he promoted education and the press during his first term from 1847 to 1849 and served again as president from 1866 to 1868.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jose-maria-castro-madriz.jpg" alt="José María Castro Madriz" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Aquiles Bigot · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jos%C3%A9_Mar%C3%ADa_Castro_Madriz.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Castro was born in San José on 1 September 1818, the son of Ramón Castro y Ramírez and Lorenza Madriz Cervantes. He studied at the University of León in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicaragua">Nicaragua</a>, where he graduated as bachelor of philosophy and doctor of law. Before his presidency he already held public office, presiding over the Congress of Deputies in 1844 and 1845. He married Pacífica Fernández, who designed the 1848 version of the Costa Rican flag that he adopted as president.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>Castro was indirectly elected president in 1847 and, inaugurated at 29, was the youngest person ever to hold the office. During this first presidency, which formally ended <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/costa-rica">Costa Rica</a>'s remaining ties to the defunct <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/federal-republic-of-central-america">Federal Republic of Central America</a>, he resigned in 1849 under the threat of a coup. His constitutional reform of 1848 nonetheless set the most restricted suffrage that independent Costa Rica ever had. He was again indirectly made president in 1866 and resigned under military pressure in 1868.</p><p>A liberal and a Freemason, he believed in the principles of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/age-of-enlightenment">Enlightenment</a> and defended freedom of the press when many governments practised censorship. He argued for the abolition of the death penalty, for antimilitarism and for free compulsory education, and was consistently critical of the political influence of the Roman Catholic Church. Lacking a committed political base, he was an easy target for the military on both occasions.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Over more than four decades Castro served as cabinet minister and ambassador, and for sixteen years as rector of the national university he had helped to create. He was chief judge of the Supreme Court of Justice from 1860 to 1866 and from 1870 to 1873, and presided over the fourth Constitutional Convention in 1859, so that he and Ricardo Jiménez alone have headed all three branches of government. In the government of his brother-in-law, President Próspero Fernández, from 1882 to 1885, he was the most influential minister and largely responsible for its anti-clerical laws. He died on 4 April 1892.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “José María Castro Madriz” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Having left the Central American federation in 1838, Costa Rica formally declared itself a republic on 31 August 1848 under President <b>José María Castro Madriz</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/republic-of-costa-rica">Republic of Costa Rica</a></li><li><blockquote>On 31 August 1848 <b>José María Castro Madriz</b> proclaimed the Republic of Costa Rica.</blockquote><a href="/state/state-of-costa-rica">State of Costa Rica</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-jose-costa-rica">San José</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/costa-rica">Costa Rica</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-jose-costa-rica">San José</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/costa-rica">Costa Rica</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=9.93,-84.08&amp;year=1866" aria-label="San José on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of José María Castro Madriz"><image href="/maps/globes/g27000.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5498 4155h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5498 4155h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=9.93,-84.08&amp;year=1866">What was here in 1866? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
